{"type":"message","id":"871b5334","parentId":"1513f1cf","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:31:32.658Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_Ioy8BmQ7gmQoBg0CTF2TZ6Ie","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"bitcoin-bond-selloff-liquidations-2026-05-18\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"Bitcoin Slides Below $77K Amid Bond Sell-Off and Macro Risk-Off\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Bitcoin selloff\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Bitcoin slides below $77,000 as Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattles risk assets\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"CoinDesk reported that Bitcoin slipped below $77,000 after President Trump\\u2019s Iran warning rattled risk assets, with oil moving higher and broad crypto liquidations following.\",\n    \"Decrypt said Bitcoin traded around $76,770 and that total crypto market liquidations exceeded $672 million, while the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63%, a 16-month high.\",\n    \"Decrypt also said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs posted $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, the largest weekly exit since late January, underscoring the institutional transmission channel from rates into crypto.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\\u2019s market roundup said Bitcoin started the week with a dip toward new May lows as ongoing BTC price pressure included collapsing U.S. bond markets and Iran-war uncertainty.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"Bitcoin slid sharply at the start of the week as macro pressure, rising Treasury yields, and geopolitical anxiety hit risk assets across markets. CoinDesk described the move as Bitcoin falling below $77,000 after U.S. President Donald Trump warned Iran that the \\\"clock is ticking,\\\" a line that pushed oil higher and helped trigger broad crypto liquidations. The same feed also framed the day as a wider crypto unwind, noting that ether sank alongside Bitcoin. A separate CoinDesk headline tied the move to a broader liquidation wave, saying crypto traders betting on a rally lost $563 million in liquidations, with ether and Bitcoin suffering the most. Another CoinDesk market note said Bitcoin slid under $77,000 as oil shock and Treasury yields hit risk assets, pointing to the same macro transmission channel.\\n\\nDecrypt provided the deepest factual read on the move. Its article said Bitcoin fell to a three-week low over the weekend as a surge in U.S. Treasury yields sent spot Bitcoin ETF outflows to their worst level since late January and drove more than $670 million in liquidations across crypto markets. The piece said the 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63% on Sunday night, the highest since February 2025, and that the move was up 70 basis points since the Iran war began. It added that odds of a 2026 rate cut had collapsed to 2%, reinforcing the idea that the bond market shock was cascading into crypto through expectations for tighter financial conditions. Decrypt quoted Diego Martin, CEO of Yellow Capital, saying geopolitical shocks no longer hit crypto directly the way they once did; instead, they hit Treasury yields, which hit risk appetite, which hits ETF flows, which hits Bitcoin. That same article said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs recorded $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, compared with $622.75 million in net inflows the prior week, and that the last comparable exit was the week of January 30, when $1.49 billion left the funds.\\n\\nDecrypt also anchored the price action with specific market levels. It said Bitcoin was trading around $76,770 at the time of writing and that total crypto liquidations had exceeded $672 million according to CoinGlass. The article said the $77,000 level was the key line to watch and warned that if it broke while perpetual swap open interest remained elevated, a retest of $70,000 or lower could become a realistic scenario rather than a tail risk. Georgii Verbitski, a derivatives trader and founder of TYMIO, told Decrypt that Bitcoin\\u2019s near-term trajectory depended heavily on the AI-driven equity rally holding up, because the market lacked a strong standalone demand driver at current levels. The story also noted that Bitcoin\\u2019s muted recovery came after its lackluster reaction to the CLARITY Act advancing out of the Senate Banking Committee.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph\\u2019s coverage aligned with the same macro theme. One article called the setup a possible \\\"bull trap\\\" at $76.5K and said Bitcoin started the week with a dip toward new May lows as ongoing BTC price pressures included collapsing U.S. bond markets. Another market piece said Bitcoin slid below $79K on macro fears, with Iran war uncertainty and fixed-income market dislocation weighing on the price. CoinTelegraph also ran a bear-trap analysis saying Bitcoin had fallen below $78,000 for the first time since the start of May, but traders were still hoping for a rebound. These pieces help confirm that the market-wide selloff was not isolated to one outlet\\u2019s framing: the move was being discussed across multiple crypto desks as a combined rates, geopolitics, and liquidation event.\\n\\nThe shared picture across the sources is a risk-off crypto episode driven by three linked forces: higher Treasury yields, Iran-related geopolitical stress, and forced unwinding of leveraged positions. Bitcoin\\u2019s break below $77,000 became the focal point because it aligned with large ETF outflows, elevated liquidations, and weakening support from other macro-risk assets. Even though some analysts still characterized the move as potentially temporary or a bear trap, all three sources pointed to the same immediate reality: Bitcoin was under pressure from the bond market first, and crypto sentiment was following that lead.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779103892658}}
{"type":"message","id":"7ef94667","parentId":"871b5334","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:31:32.658Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_gbHLVqGdmFsHjoxAO4PFSEOx","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# COINOGRAPHY EDITORIAL TEMPLATE\n\nThis document is your absolute source of truth. You MUST write the article EXACTLY following the numbered sections below. Deviation is strictly prohibited.\n\n## 🔴 CRITICAL WRITING RULES (READ FIRST)\n- **NO EM-DASHES:** Never use the `—` character anywhere in the article. Replace with a comma, a period, or restructure the sentence.\n- **NATURAL WRITING:** Never use AI phrases: \"it's worth noting\", \"it is important to note\", \"delve into\", \"in conclusion\", \"furthermore\", \"moreover\", \"in summary\", \"the crypto landscape\", \"the world of crypto\", \"a testament to\", \"shed light on\".\n- **TONE:** Direct. Trader-focused. Analytical. No hype, no fluff, no adjectives like \"amazing\" or \"shocking.\"\n- **PARAGRAPHS:** 1–4 sentences max. Break aggressively. One idea per paragraph.\n- **BULLET LISTS:** 3–4 items only. Used inside H3 sections only. Never in the conclusion or opening.\n- **ANCHOR LINKS:** You MUST naturally embed 2-3 markdown links inside the body text. These links MUST point to the exact source URLs provided in your RESEARCH_JSON. Example: `[according to recent data from Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/...)`\n- **CHART SOURCES:** The orchestrator will provide the charts. Simply embed exactly 2 mentions of the `[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]` tag inside the H2 sections.\n- **KEYWORD PLACEMENT:** \n  - Primary keyword in H1 (first 5 words)\n  - Primary keyword in first 100 words (opening hook)\n  - Primary keyword in at least one H2 and the Conclusion\n  - Secondary keywords must appear in H2 headings and their body paragraphs.\n\n## 🛠️ PRE-FLIGHT CHECKLIST\nBefore generating any text, in your `<thinking>` block, confirm:\n- [ ] I have the primary and secondary keywords.\n- [ ] I have the source URLs from the `RESEARCH_JSON` input.\n- [ ] I will write EXACTLY in this order: META → Hook → H2#1 → H2#2 → Conclusion → FAQs → Sources.\n- [ ] I will write NOTHING outside this order.\n\n============================\nARTICLE STRUCTURE (STRICT)\n============================\n\n### SECTION 1 (REQUIRED): META BLOCK\nOutput this exact block at the very top. No conversational filler before it. The very first word must be \"META\".\n- SEO Title: [50–60 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: Primary Keyword + Brand Name + Value]\n- Meta Description: [150–160 characters maximum — MUST follow formula: What the article offers + Unique angle + Call to Action]\n- URL Slug: [lowercase-hyphens-only, e.g. bitcoin-price-hits-new-all-time-high]\n- Categories: [e.g. Bitcoin News, Latest News]\n- Primary Keyword: [e.g. \"Bitcoin price drop\"]\n- Secondary Keywords: [e.g. \"BTC resistance\", \"crypto market pullback\", \"$80K Bitcoin\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 2 (REQUIRED): OPENING HOOK\n**# [H1 Title — must contain primary keyword in first 5 words, include a price or number]**\n\n*(Do NOT add an H2 heading above these paragraphs)*\nPara 1: State the core event with a specific price or data point. 2–3 sentences. Start with the primary keyword naturally. Be direct.\nPara 2: Market reaction or supporting data. One additional observation (volume, altcoins, funding rates). 2–3 sentences.\nPara 3: One-line reframe that sets the article's tone. (\"This isn't panic. It looks like traders taking money off the table after a strong run.\")\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 3 (REQUIRED): FIRST H2 SECTION\n**## [Secondary Keyword 1]: [What It Means / Signals / Shows]**\n\n[1–2 intro paragraphs explaining the news at a high level.]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 1: e.g. \"Profit-Taking Shows Up at Resistance\"]**\n[1–2 short paragraphs]\n[Optional 3-item bullet list]\n\n**### [Sub-angle 2: e.g. \"Altcoins Start Losing Steam\"]**\n[2–3 very short paragraphs — 1 to 3 sentences each]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 4 (REQUIRED): SECOND H2 SECTION\n**## Key [Levels / Data Signals / Numbers] Traders Should Watch [Now / Next]**\n\n[1 intro paragraph]\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 1: e.g. \"$75K Needs to Hold\"]**\n[1 paragraph — what this level means, what happens if it holds/breaks]\n\n**### [Level/Signal 2: e.g. \"$80K Remains the Barrier\"]**\n[1 paragraph]\n[Required 3-item bullet list starting with \"Look for:\"]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 5 (REQUIRED): CONCLUSION\n**## Conclusion**\n\nPara 1: Restate the core event in one sentence using the primary keyword. Name the key level or data point.\nPara 2: What it means for the near-term structure. One or two clean sentences.\nPara 3: Actionable close for the reader. What they should do or watch. End on a direct, confident note.\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 6 (REQUIRED): FAQS\n**## FAQs**\n\n**1. Why did [primary event] happen?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger [correction/rally/trend]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**3. What [levels/signals] should traders watch now?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**4. How does this affect [related asset/market sector]?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n[3–4 sentence answer. Target ~60 words.]\n\n---\n\n### SECTION 7 (REQUIRED): SOURCES FOOTER\n**Sources:**\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n- [Publication Name]: [source URL from research facts]\n\nSTOP GENERATING HERE. DO NOT ADD ANY BROADER CONTEXT SECTIONS. \nState your final word count on the last line of your output as: [Word Count: XXXX]\n\n## 🛡️ POST-OUTPUT VERIFICATION\nBefore finalizing your generation, mentally verify:\n[ ] Exactly 1 H1, 2 H2s, 4 H3s, Conclusion, FAQs, Sources?\n[ ] No em-dashes anywhere?\n[ ] Primary keyword in H1 first 5 words?\n[ ] 2-3 anchor links embedded in body text pointing to source URLs?\n[ ] Word count stated on final line?\n[ ] No sections added beyond the 7 defined above?\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779103892658}}
{"type":"message","id":"2a59fc7b","parentId":"7ef94667","timestamp":"2026-05-18T11:32:15.277Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_fxgaOTru1PB6ExzNzR8Ej639","name":"write","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-article-raw.md","content":"META\n- SEO Title: Bitcoin selloff | Coinography market breakdown\n- Meta Description: Track Bitcoin's drop below $77K, liquidation pressure, and yield-driven ETF outflows. See the key levels traders should watch next.\n- URL Slug: bitcoin-selloff-below-77000-liquidations-yields\n- Categories: Bitcoin News, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: Bitcoin selloff\n- Secondary Keywords: crypto liquidations, Treasury yields, spot Bitcoin ETF outflows\n\n# Bitcoin selloff sends BTC below $77,000 as liquidations top $672M\n\nBitcoin selloff pressure pushed the market below $77,000 at the start of the week, with BTC trading around $76,770 after macro risk hit across assets. The move followed a bond market sell-off and fresh geopolitical stress tied to President Trump's warning to Iran, which helped lift oil and drag on risk sentiment.\n\nThe reaction was broad, not isolated to Bitcoin. Total crypto liquidations moved past $672 million, while ether also fell and leveraged longs were forced out as traders cut exposure into rising Treasury yields and weaker ETF demand.\n\nThis looks less like a standalone crypto panic and more like a macro-driven reset in positioning.\n\n## Bitcoin selloff and crypto liquidations: What It Means\n\nThe current Bitcoin selloff lines up with a clear chain reaction. [CoinDesk reported](https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets) that BTC slipped under $77,000 as Trump's Iran warning rattled risk assets, while [Decrypt noted](https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off) that Treasury yields, ETF outflows, and liquidation pressure all hit at the same time.\n\nThat matters because the market is no longer reacting only to crypto-specific headlines. Rising funding stress in fixed income is feeding directly into ETF flows, leverage, and spot demand, which leaves BTC more exposed when rates move fast.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### Bond market pressure is moving into crypto\n\nThe 10-year U.S. Treasury yield reached 4.63%, its highest level in 16 months, according to Decrypt's market recap. At the same time, odds of a 2026 rate cut reportedly dropped to 2%, which tightened the macro backdrop for risk assets across the board.\n\nThat shift explains why traders are focused on bond markets before they look at crypto charts. When yields rise this fast, capital gets more selective and speculative longs lose support.\n\n- Higher yields raise the hurdle for risk assets.\n- Oil strength adds inflation pressure back into the picture.\n- Leveraged crypto positioning becomes easier to unwind.\n\n### ETF outflows and altcoin weakness confirm the tone\n\nSpot Bitcoin ETF outflows added another layer of pressure. Decrypt said U.S. spot Bitcoin ETFs saw $1 billion in net outflows for the week ending May 15, a sharp reversal from the prior week's $622.75 million in net inflows.\n\nThat is a meaningful signal because ETF demand has been one of the cleanest institutional support channels for Bitcoin this year. When that flow turns negative during a rates spike, traders have fewer reasons to defend support aggressively.\n\nAltcoins also failed to absorb the pressure. Ether fell with Bitcoin, and the liquidation wave shows the move was broad rather than limited to one large BTC unwind.\n\n[CoinTelegraph wrote](https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound) that the setup looked tied to macro fears, fixed-income dislocation, and Iran-war uncertainty. That cross-market framing matches what price action is showing now.\n\n## Key Treasury yield and ETF data signals traders should watch next\n\nThe most important question now is whether macro pressure eases before Bitcoin loses another support shelf. If yields stay elevated and ETF flows remain weak, traders will keep treating rebounds as relief moves rather than fresh trend confirmation.\n\n[CHART_PLACEHOLDER]\n\n### $77,000 needs to recover quickly\n\nThe $77,000 area has become the first near-term decision point. Decrypt flagged that zone as the key line to watch, and a failure to reclaim it while perpetual swap open interest stays elevated raises the risk of a deeper move toward $70,000 or lower. If BTC gets back above that level and liquidations cool, the market can start rebuilding structure instead of trading straight defense.\n\n### Treasury yields remain the real trigger\n\nRates are driving the tape more than crypto-native news right now. Even with regulatory headlines in the background, traders are watching whether the bond sell-off keeps tightening financial conditions and draining risk appetite from ETF buyers and leveraged participants.\n\nLook for:\n- The 10-year yield to move away from or hold above 4.63%.\n- Spot Bitcoin ETF flows to stabilize after the $1 billion weekly outflow.\n- BTC price response around $77,000, with $70,000 as the deeper downside reference if pressure accelerates.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Bitcoin selloff turned more serious once BTC lost $77,000 and liquidation totals climbed past $672 million. That combination matters because it links weak price action to leverage stress and deteriorating macro conditions.\n\nNear-term structure now depends less on a single crypto catalyst and more on whether Treasury yields cool and ETF selling slows. If those pressures stay in place, upside attempts may continue to fade quickly.\n\nTraders should watch yields, ETF flow data, and whether BTC can reclaim lost ground before assuming the low is in. Until that changes, treat rallies as tests, not proof.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. Why did Bitcoin selloff pressure happen?**\n\nBitcoin fell as rising Treasury yields, geopolitical tension around Iran, and forced liquidation activity hit risk assets together. The bond market sell-off raised the cost of risk and weakened sentiment across crypto. ETF outflows then reinforced the move by removing a key source of institutional demand. That mix created a fast downside cascade.\n\n**2. Is this the start of a bigger correction?**\n\nIt could become one if BTC fails to recover key support and macro pressure stays elevated. The biggest issue is that yields and ETF outflows are both moving in the wrong direction at the same time. That leaves the market vulnerable to follow-through selling. A stabilization in rates would reduce that risk.\n\n**3. What levels and signals should traders watch now?**\n\nThe first level is $77,000, which has shifted from support to a recovery test. After that, traders need to monitor the 10-year Treasury yield near 4.63% and spot Bitcoin ETF flow data for signs of stabilization. If yields keep rising and ETF outflows persist, downside risk increases. A deeper move toward $70,000 becomes more realistic in that setup.\n\n**4. How does this affect the broader crypto market?**\n\nThe move is already spilling into the rest of the market through liquidation pressure and weaker altcoin performance. Ether also fell during the sell-off, showing the drop was not limited to Bitcoin alone. When BTC weakens on macro stress, traders usually cut exposure across majors and higher-beta tokens. That tends to suppress short-term rebound attempts elsewhere.\n\n**5. What is the historical context for this move?**\n\nDecrypt said the latest ETF outflow week was the worst since late January, with the last comparable exit reaching $1.49 billion in the week of January 30. That makes this drop notable because it combines a major flow reversal with a new yield spike. The 10-year Treasury yield also hit its highest level since February 2025. Those conditions make this more than a routine intraday dip.\n\n**6. What are the short-term risks for investors?**\n\nThe main risk is assuming Bitcoin can bounce on its own while macro conditions are still deteriorating. Elevated open interest, weak ETF flows, and higher yields can produce another forced flush if support fails again. Geopolitical headlines are also still part of the risk backdrop. Position sizing and patience matter more than prediction here.\n\n**Sources:**\n- CoinDesk: https://www.coindesk.com/markets/2026/05/18/bitcoin-slides-below-usd77-000-as-trump-s-iran-warning-rattles-risk-assets\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368156/bitcoin-slides-under-77k-as-crypto-liquidations-top-672m-amid-bond-sell-off\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/markets/bitcoin-slides-below-79k-macro-fears-can-fixed-income-outflows-save-it?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\n\n[Word Count: 1134]"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4","usage":{"input":3253,"output":3043,"cacheRead":6656,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":12952,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779103892658,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DgqU41K77dEN7HUjJrHztlr0HsLHB"}}
